cartridge "SPACE ARENA/COMPUTE!'S GAZETTE/1986"

' SPACE ARENA
' A ColecoVision game based on "Space Arena", COMPUTE!'s Gazette January
' 1986, p. 48, by Bryan Files (Commodore 64 machine language, SPACE ARENA
' at $C000, started by the BASIC loader SPACE ARENA.BOOT with SYS 49152). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made
' by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a line-by-line port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' machine code, orig/sarena.dis, and watched in jsc64), the game is built
' for the ColecoVision: arrowhead ships in 16 headings, cratered rocks, a
' starfield, sound effects and a title tune.
'
' The game (as the C64 code plays it): two ships duel in a walled arena
' with four rocks in a column in the middle. The stick turns a ship one
' of 16 headings left or right and up thrusts; a ship keeps drifting (no
' friction) and bounces off the walls. The button fires a shot that
' flies 128 ticks, bouncing off the walls; a player has one shot at a time
' and can fire again only when the shot's 128 ticks are over, even if it
' hit something. A shot that hits the other ship scores 1 point and kicks
' it (half the shot's speed is added to the ship's); a shot that hits a
' rock gives it 1/16 of the shot's speed. A ship that touches a rock
' explodes, the rock takes 1/16 of its speed, and the OTHER player scores
' 20 (as the article says; the C64 code adds $20 to the high byte of
' the BCD score, a slip that gives 2000); after
' the burst every ship, rock and shot goes back to its start. Ships pass
' through each other, rocks through rocks. Speeds are capped at 64 on
' each axis; a speed below 3 does not move. Steering and thrust are read
' every 7 ticks. The match lasts the chosen time (1-5 minutes, the C64's
' F5) at the chosen speed (1-3: 48, 60 or 80 ticks a second, the C64's
' F3 and its CIA timer); higher score wins.
'
' Additions: a ONE PLAYER mode against the computer (the C64 game is for
' two players only). The computer flies the green ship through the same
' controls as a player (it turns every 7 ticks, thrusts and fires like
' anyone) and looks at the field every 12 ticks at speed 1 (8 at 2-3). It flies away from a
' rock that is close, brakes when its drift heads for a rock or gets
' fast, keeps to its side of the rocks, steps out of the row of a fast
' blue ship, and fires only when lined up exactly (within 140 pixels at
' speed 1, 180 at 2-3); at speed 3 it looks further ahead and brakes
' earlier,
' leading the shot by the blue ship's drift.
'
' Liberties: the arena is 216 x 160 pixels (the C64's about 300 x 180),
' positions in 1/64 pixel on both axes (the C64 used 1/48 across, 1/64
' down), start places scaled; box collision tests with boxes the size of
' the drawn shapes (the C64 used the sprite collision register, then its
' own boxes); the crash bonus is the article's 20, not the code's 2000
' (scores keep 5 digits, the C64 showed 4); the crashed player's bar blinks at 2.5 Hz (the C64
' blinked it at about 7 Hz); the C64's thrust hiss is a short repeated
' noise; the options are a joystick menu on the title (the C64 used F1,
' F3, F5); at the end the winner is named, then the title (the C64 went
' back to its option screen).
'
' Controls: controller 1 flies the blue ship, controller 2 the green ship
' (in ONE PLAYER mode either controller flies the blue ship). Stick left
' and right turn, up thrusts, either button fires. On the title, up and
' down choose a line, left and right change it, a button starts (or shows
' the credits).
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

const TSt0 = 128
const TSt1 = 129
const TSt2 = 130
const TSt3 = 131
const TBarb = 132
const TBarg = 133
const TTlb = 134
const TTlg = 135
const SShip0 = 0
const SShip1 = 4
const SShip2 = 8
const SShip3 = 12
const SShip4 = 16
const SShip5 = 20
const SShip6 = 24
const SShip7 = 28
const SShip8 = 32
const SShip9 = 36
const SShip10 = 40
const SShip11 = 44
const SShip12 = 48
const SShip13 = 52
const SShip14 = 56
const SShip15 = 60
const SRock = 64
const SShot = 68
const SBur0 = 72
const SBur1 = 76
const NTiles8 = 64
const SprBytes = 640
const SndTuneA = 1
const SndTuneB = 2
const SndShot = 3
const SndHit = 4
const SndBoom = 5
const SndThrust = 6
const SndStart = 7
const SndOver = 8
const NSpr = 8
const BoomLen = 100
const BoomQuiet = 70
const ShotLen = 128

' per object (0 blue ship, 1 green ship, 2-5 rocks, 6 blue shot, 7 green
' shot): the centre's limits (pixels 24-231, 27-175 for ships; 25-231,
' 27-176 rocks; 20-235, 22-179 shots) and the half sizes of its box
' (here x 64, in the positions' 1/64 pixel units)
data LoX words 1536,1536,1600,1600,1600,1600,1280,1280
data HiX words 14784,14784,14784,14784,14784,14784,15040,15040
data LoY words 1728,1728,1728,1728,1728,1728,1408,1408
data HiY words 11200,11200,11264,11264,11264,11264,11456,11456
data HalfW bytes 6,6,7,7,7,7,2,2
data HalfH bytes 6,6,6,6,6,6,2,2
' the start: centres in pixels
data StartX bytes 26,229,128,128,128,128,0,0
data StartY bytes 100,100,45,82,118,155,0,0
' thrust per heading (0 up, clockwise): DirY[d], and DirY[d + 4] across
' (negative bytes are stored unsigned: read them through an i8)
data DirY bytes -6,-5,-4,-2,0,2,4,5,6,5,4,2,0,-2,-4,-5,-6,-5,-4,-2
' ticks a frame x 60, by speed 1-3 (48, 60, 80 ticks a second)
data TickAdd bytes 48,60,80
' frames between the computer's looks at the field, by speed
data AiGap bytes 15,8,6
' how far ahead it looks (ticks), how near a rock may come, how fast it
' may drift, how far it fires, by speed
data AiLook bytes 128,96,128
data AiNearX bytes 46,38,46
data AiNearY bytes 42,34,42
data AiFast bytes 36,44,36
data AiRange bytes 190,180,180

record Obj:
  i16 X
  i16 Y
  i8 Vx
  i8 Vy
end record

